import Dregg2.Crypto.CryptoFloorTeeth       -- FALSIFIABILITY TEETH for the crypto hardness floors + the PROPER (adversary-indexed) floor. The Boolean floors are EXISTENCE-REFUTATIONS (Lattice.MSISHard := ¬∃solution, MLWESearchHard := ¬∃(s,e), SchnorrDLHard := ¬DLSolver), degenerate at real params — so every theorem conditioned on them is discharged by a vacuous hypothesis, invisible to #assert_axioms. §1 not_msisHard_of_short_ball: MSISHard is FALSE at a compressing A — the β₀-short ball outnumbers the codomain, two short vectors collide (pigeonhole), their difference is a short nonzero kernel vector (nrm_sub_le) = a genuine IsMSISSolution; fires on [id|1] over ZMod 5 (25>5), witness (4,1) exhibited via selftarget_extract_nonzero. §2 not_mlweSearchHard_of_sample: MLWE is FALSE for any genuine public key (the short (s,e) IS the secret key, exists by construction). §3 schnorrDLHard_of_smul_collision: SchnorrDLHard is TRIVIALLY TRUE at FINITE params (DLSolver demands solve(sk·G)=sk for ALL sk:ℕ; on a finite group sk↦sk·G is non-injective so DLSolver is UNSATISFIABLE) — opposite vacuity flavor, satisfied by anything finite; the infinite toy (SchnorrCurveField.toy_dl_not_hard) is the refutable pole. §4 msisHardQuant_solverAdv_iff_msisHard: FloorBridge.msisSolverAdv indexes the quant floor by SOLUTIONS, so MSISHardQuantShape(msisSolverAdv A β) ↔ MSISHard A β — the SAME bug in probabilistic costume, false at compressing (msisHardQuant_solverAdv_augmented_id_false). §5 THE PROPER FLOOR: keep the MSISHardQuantShape SHAPE (∀s, Negl(adv s)) but re-instantiate adv over a GENUINE resource-bounded adversary family with a λ-DECAYING advantage ENSEMBLE (a single-instance ∃-adversary collapses to ∃-solution via hardcoding, so it must be λ-indexed) — guessAdv=1/2^λ SATISFIES (negl_two_pow), constant-1 REFUTES (not_negl_one): non-vacuous AND non-trivial. §6 dregg_pq_game_forger_negl_under_comp_floor: the DEPLOYED dreggPqSigScheme keystone re-grounded via ForkingDischarge.game_forger_negl_under_msis_quant on the adversary-indexed floor (NOT the vacuous msisSolverAdv); deployed_boolean_floor_refuted shows the OLD Boolean floor of pq_advantage_bounded_under_msis is FALSE at compressing; FIRES (zeroFamily) + floor LOAD-BEARING (const25). #assert_all_clean (16 keystones), no sorry.
import Dregg2.Crypto.FloorGames -- ⚑ THE *HardQuant REPAIR (07-16): the five content-free floors RESTATED as the standard cryptographic games, with the problem IN the statement. §1 ONE SCHEMA, MANY PROBLEMS: a Game carries a λ-indexed finite Inst space, an Ans space and a WIN RELATION; Adversary G := run : ∀ l, Inst l → Ans l; gameAdv = ProbCrypto.winProb of the win event; Hard G Eff := ∀ A, Eff A → Negl (gameAdv G A). The five floors are this schema at FIVE DIFFERENT games — MSISHardQuant F Eff (wins := Lattice.IsMSISSolution, msisGame_wins_iff by Iff.rfl), MLWEHardQuant (wins := recovering the actual secret; mlweFamily_isSample PROVES every instance is a Lattice.IsMLWESample), DLHardQuant (wins := x·G = the sampled challenge point), HashCRHardQuant (wins := a genuine collision, reusing HashFloorHonesty.KeyedHashFamily/CollisionFinder), DecisionMLWEHardQuant (the LWE-vs-uniform gap, real world PINNED to A·s+e, ONE distinguisher applied to both worlds). So the sweep's defect is gone at the TYPE level: a HashCRHardQuant proof is Hard at a different Game and no longer elaborates in an MSISHardQuant slot (HardQuantVacuity §6 pins it with fail_if_success + the positive pole). §2 THE COLLAPSE — hard_top_iff_solvableFrac_negl: Hard G ⊤ ↔ Negl (solvableFrac G). At the UNRESTRICTED adversary class a game floor IS the probabilistic EXISTENCE floor — the Classical.choice adversary (choiceAdv, a perfectly good Adversary: nothing in that type bounds resources) wins on exactly the solvable instances. So the sweep's Horn A is NOT an artifact of msisSolverAdv or of the old shape: it is a theorem about EVERY unrestricted-adversary formulation, this one included (msisHardQuant_top_false_of_compressing, mlweHardQuant_top_false, dlHardQuant_top_false — the last two unconditionally). ⚑ AND IT REFUTES THE REPAIR THE SWEEP ITSELF PRESCRIBED: HashFloorHonesty.CollisionResistant F WAS HashCRHardQuant F ⊤ under a name that hid the ⊤ (proved by collisionResistant_iff_hashCRHardQuant_top; ⚑ 07-28 BOTH are DELETED — the def, and the iff whose only job was to see through the name — and every carrier is restated at (fun _ => True) written out), so hashCRHardQuant_top_false_of_compressing proves HashFloorHonesty's 'proper keyed computational floor' — named in VACUITY-SWEEP.md as 'the correct pattern already in the tree' — is itself FALSE at deployed parameters, by the same pigeonhole, one Classical.choice later (mod2Family_not_hashCRHardQuant_top fires it on the tree's own compressing example). Copying that pattern would have been the FOURTH costume. §8 THE ESCAPE, AND THE RESIDUAL NAMED: since §2 is an ↔, no restatement of the win relation escapes — only Eff does, and the tree HAS NO COST MODEL. Eff is carried as a PARAMETER with both poles PROVED (⊤ ⇒ false at deployed params; ⊥ ⇒ vacuous, hard_bot_vacuous); solvableIsAFiniteSearch shows Eff := Computable cannot help (every answer space is a Fintype, so brute force is total computable — what disqualifies it is COST, which Mathlib cannot state over an arbitrary carrier). What the repair buys, exactly: problem in the statement, five distinct floors, consumers must EXHIBIT a reduction, and the dilemma localized to ONE named object. #assert_all_clean (18 keystones).
import Dregg2.Crypto.HardQuantVacuity -- ⚠ VACUITY SWEEP (07-16): the *HardQuant floor FAMILY carries NO problem content. §1 the_five_floors_are_one_prop: MSISHardQuantShape/MLWEHardQuantShape/DLHardQuantShape/HashCRHardQuantShape/DecisionMLWEHardQuantShape are ONE Prop (∀ s, Negl (adv s)) — five names, Iff.rfl four ways; nothing in any of them mentions a lattice, a curve, a hash or IsMSISSolution, and DecisionMLWEHardQuant's own doc says the 'INTENDED' adv is a DecisionFamily.adv (intended, never enforced — the CoCurvilinearity docstring-constraint defect one level up). §2 the consumers are HYPOTHESIS APPLICATION: every re-grounded keystone has shape (adv)(s)(hfloor : <X>HardQuant adv) : Negl (adv s), which is `hfloor s`, a P → P instantiation — the_vrf_keystone_accepts_the_hash_floor CALLS the real VrfRegrounded.lattice_vrf_uniqueness_advantage_bound passing a HashCRHardQuantShape proof into the slot its signature declares MSISHardQuantShape, and it TYPECHECKS. §3 THE DILEMMA (both horns from the tree's own lemmas): horn A — tie adv to MSIS via the only MSIS-indexed adv in the tree (FloorBridge.msisSolverAdv) and msisHardQuant_solverAdv_iff_msisHard collapses the floor to the Boolean MSISHard, FALSE at compressing params, so every consumer is VACUOUS; horn B — leave adv untied (guessAdv = 1/2^λ, the tree's OWN non-vacuity witness, mentions no A/β/IsMSISSolution) and MSISHardQuantShape holds SIMULTANEOUSLY with ¬MSISHard (augmented id 1) 0 — the floor is satisfied in a world where its named problem is refuted. No third instantiation exists. §4 the METHOD tooth: SheepCountingHardQuant (name chosen to mean nothing) passes CryptoFloorTeeth.proper_floor_is_genuine's 'satisfiable AND refutable' test verbatim AND is definitionally MSISHardQuantShape (Iff.rfl) — so that test measures the SHAPE of a predicate over an arbitrary adv, never whether a floor is ABOUT its named problem. Affected consumers (statement-identical): VrfRegrounded.lattice_vrf_uniqueness_advantage_bound/_with_guessing_bound, ThreadAdvantageBound.forger_advantage_bound_under_msis/_with_challenge_bound/decision_distinguisher_advantage_bound/lossy_id_advantage_bound. NOT claimed: no downstream theorem is FALSE — they are all true, for reasons unrelated to their names. Repair (named, not applied): index adv by a resource-bounded adversary against the actual problem RELATION, so the floor is neither the Boolean floor (horn A) nor problem-free (horn B). #assert_axioms-clean, sorry-free.
import Dregg2.Crypto.RomOracle -- ⚑ THE COST-MODEL ESCAPE, part 1/3 (07-16): the SYNTAX of a random-oracle adversary — one that can only learn the oracle BY QUERYING IT. OracleComp D R A is a decision tree (pure a | query d k) that branches ONLY on answers it asked for; QueryBounded Q M is an INDUCTIVE, SYNTACTIC bound on the calls along every path (it quantifies over all answers, so it depends on no oracle). eval/queried run and instrument it. ⚑ THE DETERMINATION THEOREM eval_congr_of_agree_on_queried: two oracles agreeing on M.queried H are INDISTINGUISHABLE to M — same path, same output. That converts the syntactic bound into a constraint on BEHAVIOUR, and it is exactly what FloorGames.choiceAdv (which reads the whole instance) does not obey. Teeth: ofList exhibits real members (query a list, return a function of the answers, ofList_eval); queryBounded_zero_pure inhabits the class; queryBounded_zero_const proves the budget is a GENUINE restriction (a 0-query computation's output is constant in the oracle). #assert_all_clean (13 keystones).
import Dregg2.Crypto.RomCounting -- ⚑ THE COST-MODEL ESCAPE, part 2/3 (07-16): LAZY SAMPLING AS PURE FINITE COUNTING — no measure theory. A uniform random oracle IS a uniform element of the finite type D → R, so conditioning is a Finset: cyl S σ = the oracles agreeing with the partial assignment σ on S, condProb C win = the fraction of C that wins (cyl_empty/condProb_cyl_empty tie it back to ProbCrypto.winProb, so this composes with the existing probability core). The workhorses: cyl_filter_eq (slicing a cylinder at a FRESH d ∉ S gives the cylinder over insert d S), cyl_insert_card_eq (the |R| slices are EQUINUMEROUS, by the bijection H ↦ Function.update H d r'), cyl_card_insert_mul, condProb_split (⚑ THE LAW OF TOTAL PROBABILITY over a fresh coordinate), and condProb_fresh_eq: ⚑ A FRESH POINT HITS A FIXED TARGET WITH PROBABILITY EXACTLY 1/|R| — what the adversary has not queried, it does not know. That single lemma is the whole information-theoretic content of the ROM bound in part 3. No [Nonempty R] needed anywhere: every fresh-point lemma takes σ : D → R and a point of D, so σ d already inhabits R. #assert_all_clean (16 keystones).
import Dregg2.Crypto.RomQueryFloor -- ⚑⚑⚑ THE COST-MODEL ESCAPE, part 3/3 — A FLOOR THAT IS PROVED, NOT ASSUMED (07-16). FloorGames §8 named the residual: Eff has no content because the tree has no cost model, and hard_top_iff_solvableFrac_negl proves NOTHING BUT Eff can escape. ⚑ THE ESCAPE IS NOT A COMPLEXITY CLASS — IT IS THE RANDOM ORACLE. romCollisionGame F: the instance IS a uniformly sampled oracle H : D l → R l, the win relation IS a genuine collision. RomEff F Q (the Eff): the adversaries that FACTOR THROUGH a QueryBounded (Q l) tree — a predicate on the SAME Adversary type Hard quantifies over, so this is the tree's existing floor schema at a class with real content. §3 ⚑ birthday_cond/birthday_bound PROVE, by induction on the query tree over RomCounting's counting lemmas, the classic UNCONDITIONAL bound: a Q-query adversary finds a collision with probability ≤ (Q²+1)/|R|. Nothing is assumed under it — no MSIS, no MLWE, no 'hash is collision resistant'; the invariant is that what the adversary has LEARNED stays collision-free, and it pays 1 whenever that breaks. §6 romCollision_hard: at |R l| = 2^l and a polynomially-bounded budget the floor is TRUE (Negl from negl_two_pow — the digest space outruns the budget). §7 ⚑ BOTH ESCAPES PROVED, not asserted: NOT ⊤ — choiceAdv_not_romEff proves Classical.choice's adversary is EXCLUDED (it would have to know a collision it never asked for), romEff_not_iff_solvableFrac_negl proves the COLLAPSE ↔ FAILS for RomEff, romEff_ne_top; NOT ⊥ — twoPointAdv_in_romEff exhibits a member that genuinely uses its queries and twoPointAdv_gameAdv_eq computes its advantage EXACTLY 1/|R l| > 0. §8 binaryRomFamily (λ-bit digest, 2× domain): binaryRom_top_false (the UNRESTRICTED floor at the SAME game is FALSE, by the SAME pigeonhole that refutes HashFloorHonesty.CollisionResistant) vs binaryRom_hard_linear_budget (the query-bounded floor is TRUE) — same game, same win relation, two Eff's, opposite verdicts, which is what it means for Eff to do real work. ⚠ SCOPE, NOT HIDDEN: the budget is a QUERY count, not a running time — a Q-query adversary may do unbounded work BETWEEN queries (that is SOUND for hash-based bounds, and is why they need no computational assumption), but it restricts a lattice adversary NOT AT ALL, since MSIS/MLWE adversaries make no oracle calls. The general PPT Eff remains open and remains a parameter. Contrast ConcreteSecurity.StepBound.PPT, already in-tree: PolyBoundedNat on a bare steps : ℕ → ℕ attached to NO adversary, constraining NO behaviour — a name for a number sequence, unusable as an Eff, and used as one nowhere. #assert_all_clean (25 keystones).
import Dregg2.Crypto.RomQueryLog -- ⚑ THE QUERY-LOG SUBSTRATE (07-17, idea A2) — the one interface a STRAIGHT-LINE EXTRACTOR needs on top of RomOracle, imitating VCVio's QueryLog/withLogging in SHAPE only (mathlib-only, no VCVio dep). OracleComp.log M H = the list of (d, H d) pairs M produces under H — every queried point PAIRED with its answer, in order; OracleComp.evalLog M H = the one-pass logging run returning (result, log). Agreement with the un-instrumented substrate: evalLog_fst_eq_eval (the log run does not change the output), evalLog_snd_eq_log, bundled as evalLog_eq : M.evalLog H = (M.eval H, M.log H). ⚑ THE EXTRACTION SUBSTRATE, three facts a query-log extractor consumes: evalLog_snd_map_fst_eq_queried — the log's DOMAIN PROJECTION IS queried (the log records the very points eval_congr_of_agree_on_queried quantifies over, so log_length_eq_queried_length + QueryBounded.log_length_le make a budget on the read set and a budget on queried THE SAME bound); evalLog_fst_eq_eval; and mem_evalLog_answer — the READ-BACK, (d, r) ∈ (M.evalLog H).2 → H d = r, so the extractor recovers H's answer at any logged point from the log alone (the log records H on the queried set and records nothing false). Tooth: ofList_log computes the canonical member's log EXACTLY as ds.map (fun d => (d, H d)) — the trace is not vacuously empty. ADDITIVE: touches NO existing soundness statement; B1 (re-basing FriLdtExtractV3 over this model) is the consumer, not this file. #assert_all_clean (14 keystones).
import Dregg2.Crypto.KeyedRomFloor -- ⚑⚑⚑ THE KEYED, QUERY-COUNTED COLLISION FLOOR, and the DEATH of the counterexample (07-23). Exec.SystemRootsBindingReduction.sysRoots_floor_polyTime_false_babyBear PROVES the deployed sponge floor is FALSE at Eff := CostAdversary.IsPolyTime — the ArkLib KZG disease: IsPolyTime prices ANSWER SIZE (isPolyTime_of_polySize_answers), so a Classical.choice .pure-leaf answering a short collision is in the class and wins with prob 1. This module gives the FIXED floor as the keyed presentation of RomQueryFloor: keyedRomGame F = romCollisionGame over the tag-separated domain Key × Input (domain separation), KeyedRomEff F Q = RomEff there. keyedRom_hard: the floor is PROVED (birthday bound). ⚑ THE COUNTEREXAMPLE DIES TWO WAYS. (a) keyedChoiceAdv_excluded — the full-oracle Classical.choice answerer (the shortCollAdv analogue) is OUTSIDE KeyedRomEff (choiceAdv_not_romEff): it would have to know a collision it never queried. (b) fixedPairAdv_negl — the SHARPER mirror: the EXACT shape that refutes the IsPolyTime floor, a .pure leaf answering a FIXED short collision, IS admitted by KeyedRomEff (a 0-query tree) but over the SAMPLED oracle a fixed pair collides with prob only 1/|R| (fixedPairAdv_gameAdv_eq), NEGLIGIBLE — so the adversary that wins prob 1 against the FIXED sponge wins only negligibly against the SAMPLED oracle; sampling the key after the adversary is what collapses the advantage. Both poles at the fixed model: keyedRom_top_false (¬⊤, compressing) + twoPointKeyed_in_keyedRomEff/_gameAdv_pos (¬⊥, positive advantage). deployedKeyed_verdict: all four facts at one concrete keyed family + budget. #assert_all_clean (14 keystones).
