in-range tables at the same anchor root have the SAME TOTAL STAKE. Midnight twin closed too (`midRowWidthGates`); its `rowBlock` was already faithful, so its wound was purely hash-level. ⚠ MEASURED ALONGSIDE, NOT FIXED: `LightClientSolanaAir.lean:96-152`'s `ROOTED_STK`/`TOTAL_STK` were hidden witnesses with NO range lookup and only the slacks were ranged, at 128 bits -- SUPERSEDED 2026-08-03: both tallies are now four-limb u64 vectors and EVERY limb carries its own 16-bit lookup, so the 128-bit slack table is gone (see Dregg2.Circuit.LimbTally) — sound over the ℤ reading its lemmas use, the shared field-soundness residual over F_p; field wraparound on the tally, not an encoder alias.
import Dregg2.Bridge.MinaChainSelection -- ⚑⚑ SAMASIKA CHAIN SELECTION — and **it is a TOURNAMENT, not an order.** `select`/`is_short_range`/relative-min-window-density rendered against `proof_of_stake.ml` with every constant cited (`k` 290, `slots_per_epoch` 7140, `sub_windows_per_window` 11, derived `grace_period_end` **2237** — the spec doc's table AND openmina both say 1440 and are stale). Tie-break chain: `blockchain_length`, then Blake2b(`last_vrf_output`) bytewise, then **the state hash as a FIELD ELEMENT** (numeric in `[0,p)`, not byte-LE, not Base58). PROVED: determinism-with-content (it factors through an eight-field key by `rfl`, which typechecks ONLY because nothing else is projected), irreflexivity, ASYMMETRY (an invariant the OCaml only QUICKCHECKS, `:4617`), ties-only-on-equal-keys, and transitivity restricted to pairwise-short-range tips. ⚑⚑ `beats_not_transitive`: genuine 3-cycles (`B` beats `A`, `C` beats `B`, `A` beats `C`), `decide`-checked at REAL mainnet constants, by TWO independent mechanisms — (i) `is_short_range` partitions pairs differently, so different PAIRS are judged by different rules; and (ii) even in the long regime alone, the relative minimum window density **is not an intrinsic scalar but a function of the PAIR** via `max_slot` — `relative_density_is_pair_dependent` shows one chain measuring **70** against a rival at its own slot and **7** projected twelve slots forward. **So "the canonical chain" is not a function of a candidate SET: folding `select` over candidates is order-dependent, and an adversarial peer can walk a node around the cycle.** Hence the light-client seam `minaBetterTip` is deliberately PAIRWISE against a fixed anchored segment, with `minaBetterTip_is_not_foldable` stating that limit as a THEOREM. Off-circuit decision procedure, said out loud: Mina never proves selection in a SNARK either (the in-circuit density code is block PRODUCTION, a different function).
