import Dregg2.Bridge.MinaChainSelectionDifferential -- ⚑⚑ AND THE TWO REAL IMPLEMENTATIONS DISAGREE. Driving **openmina's own `mina_core::consensus`** over 5 real fork pairs (openmina's own test fixtures, re-asserted in Rust before emitting) plus 52 derived by moving `curr_global_slot` across sub-window and grace-period boundaries: **57/57 rows match our Lean**, and **30/57 differ from openmina in the relative density, with 8/57 differing in the FINAL VERDICT** — openmina adopts a different chain than the daemon would. Three compounding defects, each now a theorem: shift count measured in SLOTS where the daemon works in SUB-WINDOWS (and the spec's §5.4.12 contradicts its own §5.4.9); `for _ in 0..=shift_count`, one zero more than the pseudocode it transcribes; and `GRACE_PERIOD_END = 1440` against the daemon's 2237. `three_renderings_three_answers` pins ONE question at **50 (daemon) / 23 (openmina) / 28 (the spec document's own pseudocode)**. Separately, openmina's `is_short_range_fork` keys on `epoch_count` where the daemon keys on `curr_global_slot / slots_per_epoch`, which is **permanently divergent after any fully-empty epoch**, and drops the daemon's `Slot.succ`. Notably openmina's block-PRODUCTION density is a faithful port — the two paths live in the same repo and disagree. ⚠ Measured alongside: **openmina's fork fixtures no longer deserialize with openmina's own types**, so the only tests it has over its chain-selection code do not currently run; and `subWindowDensities` is NOT a field of the public GraphQL `ConsensusState`, so the long-range rule cannot be evaluated from the surface our observer uses at all — the short-range rule can. ⚑ The lane deliberately added NO `@[export]` for this, because the long-range branch needs data GraphQL cannot supply and an export would be an UN-CALLED GATE — the exact class this tree spent today closing.
import Dregg2.Bridge.MinaSlidingWindow -- ⚑ SAMASIKA'S SLIDING DENSITY WINDOW, replayed against the real chain. **849 real consecutive canonical devnet transitions, reproduced from ONE SEED and the slot sequence alone** — every block's own reported `min_window_density` AND its 11-entry `sub_window_densities`, ZERO mismatches, across two runs including an outage era where the minimum drops 34 → 14 → 8 → 3 with slot gaps up to 43. Two mutations (dropping the projection; an off-by-one ring index) are REFUTED by both runs, so the gate can go red. Off-circuit decision procedure, said out loud: pure `Nat`/`Int`/`Bool`, no AIR — Mina proves this in-circuit per block, and this is the unchecked twin a verifier runs. The slide law nobody writes down is proved exhaustively (11 ring positions × 11 shifts × 11 indices): index `i` carries sub-window `prevGsw − age i` with `age i = (prevRel + 11 − i) % 11`, and the entry survives exactly when `age i + diff ≤ 11`. ⚑ FINDING 1: **the density bound `d[i] ≤ 7` is NOT an invariant of the update function** — `step` alone yields 8 on mainnet constants, `by decide`. What preserves it is `step` COMPOSED WITH the strict slot increase that block validity enforces, so **a light client replaying the window must independently check `parent.slot < child.slot`; the density arithmetic never re-checks it.** ⚑ FINDING 2: **a backwards slot is not refused, it is MIS-COMPUTED** — the same 21-sub-window gap reads as density **77 backwards and 0 forwards**, because `overlapping` is vacuously true so stale entries are retained and the density inflated. Both proved. ⚑ FINDING 3: **`grace_period_end` is 2237, not 1440** — the written spec's constants table AND openmina's `consensus.rs:15` both say 1440; the daemon computes 2160 + 77. Two independent lanes caught this. ⚑ FINDING 4: the daemon GraphQL **cannot seed a window replay at all** — `ConsensusState` exposes `minWindowDensity` but NOT `subWindowDensities`, so this used Mina's precomputed-block archiv