import Dregg2.Bridge.MinaStateHashRealBlock -- ⚑⚑ **THE REALITY GATE FOR A TIP'S IDENTITY, AND IT TAKES NO ORACLE.** `state_hash` is not on Mina's peer-to-peer wire at all — every node computes it, which is why `get_best_tip` never sends one. But `previous_state_hash` IS, in the clear, as the first 32 bytes of every `Protocol_state.Value`. So on a CONSECUTIVE pair: `deriveStateHash(block_N) = block_{N+1}.previous_state_hash`. Both sides came from the daemon; no transcription of ours is on either side, no explorer is asked, nothing is compiled but this. A comparison against a value we or openmina computed would be a differential against another implementation — this is a differential against **the chain**. MEASURED on live devnet blocks **540221 → 540222**, captured 2026-07-30 over Mina's own protocol: the equation HOLDS, in **26 s of KERNEL** `decide` (no `native_decide`, no `sorry`, zero axioms). ⚑ That is the ONLY claim in the tree that can refute the ~1,400-bit order of `Body.to_input`, and it catches precisely what `MinaBinprotRealBlock` is structurally blind to: a flipped byte INSIDE `Blockchain_state` keeps the 1,544-byte exact fit and `select` reads nothing there, so every check in that file still passes while the preimage moves — `a_flipped_byte_in_the_blockchain_state_moves_the_identity` is that case. Both refusal polarities come free as a `∀` over every wrong hash (instantiating `MinaStateHashDerive.the_guard_accepts_the_derived_and_refuses_the_rest`), not two sampled neighbours. REGENERATE with `bridge/tools/mina-consecutive-pair.py`, do not edit.
