import Dregg2.Bridge.PicklesProofChainGate -- the `@[export] dregg_mina_proof_chain_ok` seam: the PROOF↔PROOF chain decision, and the first thing in this tree that binds a served Pickles proof to anything outside its own bytes. ⚑ WHAT IT CLOSES AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. A Wrap proof is not self-binding to its BLOCK — `messages_for_next_step_proof.app_state` is literally `()` on the wire, the verifier substitutes the block's `stateHash` as `app_state`, and that enters only public-input slot 12 of 40 (the `messages_for_next_step_proof` Poseidon digest); the other 39 slots are functions of the proof alone. Reaching slot 12 as a COMPARISON needs the six wire-DROPPED words (`combined_inner_product`, `b`, `zeta_to_srs_length`, `zeta_to_domain_size`, `perm`, `xi`), i.e. `expand_deferred`, plus a 40-point MSM and two sponges — that is docs/MINA-REAL-BLOCK-GATE.md §6, NOT this. What IS closed: Pickles recursion makes block N's Step proof verify block N−1's Wrap proof, so block N's bytes carry the parent's own IPA accumulator `sg` AND the parent's 16 IPA challenges, comparable with zero arithmetic. MEASURED on 40 consecutive real devnet blocks (539761…539800): 39/39 pairs link on BOTH fingerprints, 40/40 distinct `sg`, 0 self-naming blocks, 0 non-adjacent coincidences. `chainOk_adjacent_proofs_differ` proves the payoff — an accepted segment CANNOT serve the same proof twice in a row, which is exactly what one real proof replayed under 290 fabricated headers used to do — and `chainOk_pins_every_seam` proves every adjacency is checked, not just the first, so runs cannot be spliced, shuffled or padded. ⚠ It does NOT bind a proof to a `stateHash`: an adversary holding a genuine consecutive run can still re-label the headers. And it is still not a Mina light client — fork choice is formalized nowhere and Samasika `select` is a TOURNAMENT, not an order.
